  'botox-in-lagos-cost-and-what-to-expect', 'Botox in Lagos: what it costs and what to expect', 'What botulinum toxin actually does, what a treatment costs in Lagos, how long results last, and the questions worth asking before anyone injects you.',
  $body_json${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most people asking about Botox in Lagos want two things answered before anything else: what it costs, and whether they'll still look like themselves. Both are fair questions, and both have specific answers."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Botox actually does"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Botulinum toxin blocks the signal between a nerve and a muscle. The muscle stops contracting as hard, and the skin folding over it creases less. That's the whole mechanism."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This matters because it decides what Botox can and can't fix. Lines that show up when you frown, squint, or raise your eyebrows respond well, because a muscle is creating them. Lines sitting on your face while it's completely still are a different problem — that's volume loss or sun damage, and relaxing a muscle won't touch it. An honest injector will tell you which of the two you have, and sometimes the answer is that you need fillers, resurfacing, or better sun protection instead."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It is a prescription medicine. In Nigeria that means a licensed medical professional should be assessing you, injecting you, and available afterwards if something needs attention."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What it costs in Lagos"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Botox is priced per unit, not per face. At Reverse Aesthetics a unit is ₦12,000, and your total depends on how much muscle needs treating. A person with strong frown muscles needs more units than someone with fine, mobile skin, so two people can walk out of the same clinic having paid different amounts for the same area."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Be careful with any clinic quoting a single flat price for \"Botox\" without telling you the unit count. That's where undertreatment hides. Fewer units cost less and wear off faster, which is fine if you were told that's what you were buying."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Some treatments are priced whole because the dose is predictable:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lip flip — ₦120,000"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brow lift — ₦45,000"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gummy smile correction — ₦200,000"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Masseter slimming, for jaw width or teeth grinding — ₦300,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Masseter treatment is worth calling out because people come for the jawline and stay for the grinding. If you wake with jaw ache or your dentist has mentioned wear on your molars, relaxing that muscle treats the cause, and the narrower jaw is a side effect."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What happens at the appointment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A first appointment runs about thirty minutes, most of it talking. You'll be asked to frown, raise your brows, and smile so your injector can see which muscles are doing what, and how strong they are. Medical history matters here: pregnancy, breastfeeding, neuromuscular conditions, and some antibiotics all rule treatment out or change it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The injections themselves take a few minutes. The needle is very fine. Most people rate it well below a blood test, and no numbing is needed for standard areas. You may see small raised bumps where the product went in; those settle within twenty minutes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can drive yourself home and go back to work. There's no sedation and nothing to recover from."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first two weeks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nothing happens on day one, and people who don't know this spend the evening convinced it failed. The effect starts appearing around day three, builds through the first week, and reaches full strength at day fourteen."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That two-week mark is when your result should be reviewed. Judging it earlier is how people end up overtreated: you decide at day five that it isn't working, ask for more, and then the original dose arrives on top of the extra one."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the first four hours, stay upright and don't rub the treated area. Skip the gym, alcohol, and heat until the next day. These reduce the chance of the product spreading somewhere it wasn't meant to go."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long it lasts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three to four months is the usual range. Strong muscles need retreating sooner. So does a fast metabolism, and so does heavy training."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The return of movement is gradual. Rather than snapping back, the muscle recovers over a few weeks, which is why regular patients book their next appointment while they still have some effect left. Treating consistently over a couple of years often means needing fewer units, because a muscle that's been relaxed for long stretches gets weaker."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Choosing an injector"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Lagos market has a wide spread of skill, and price alone won't tell you where someone sits on it. Some questions worth asking:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who is injecting me, and what is their medical qualification?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many units am I getting, and in which muscles?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What product are you using, and can I see the vial?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What happens if my brow drops or one side lifts more than the other?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is the two-week review included?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That last pair matters most. Complications from Botox are usually temporary, but they need someone who recognises them and knows what to do. A dropped eyelid can be treated with prescription drops. Asymmetry can often be balanced with a few extra units. Someone who can't answer what they'd do in those situations shouldn't be holding the needle."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask to see the vial. It costs nothing, and it's the simplest check against counterfeit product, which does circulate."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When the answer is no"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A clinic that treats everyone who walks in isn't being generous. If your lines are static, if you've had a poor result elsewhere that hasn't worn off yet, or if what's bothering you is really volume or skin quality, Botox is the wrong tool and you'll have spent money to be disappointed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bring a photograph of what's bothering you if you can, and say what you want to look like rather than which treatment you want. Sometimes the answer is a smaller intervention than you expected. Sometimes it's nothing at all."}]}]}$body_json$::jsonb,
  $body_html$<p>Most people asking about Botox in Lagos want two things answered before anything else: what it costs, and whether they'll still look like themselves. Both are fair questions, and both have specific answers.</p><h2>What Botox actually does</h2><p>Botulinum toxin blocks the signal between a nerve and a muscle. The muscle stops contracting as hard, and the skin folding over it creases less. That's the whole mechanism.</p><p>This matters because it decides what Botox can and can't fix. Lines that show up when you frown, squint, or raise your eyebrows respond well, because a muscle is creating them. Lines sitting on your face while it's completely still are a different problem — that's volume loss or sun damage, and relaxing a muscle won't touch it. An honest injector will tell you which of the two you have, and sometimes the answer is that you need fillers, resurfacing, or better sun protection instead.</p><p>It is a prescription medicine. In Nigeria that means a licensed medical professional should be assessing you, injecting you, and available afterwards if something needs attention.</p><h2>What it costs in Lagos</h2><p>Botox is priced per unit, not per face. At Reverse Aesthetics a unit is ₦12,000, and your total depends on how much muscle needs treating. A person with strong frown muscles needs more units than someone with fine, mobile skin, so two people can walk out of the same clinic having paid different amounts for the same area.</p><p>Be careful with any clinic quoting a single flat price for "Botox" without telling you the unit count. That's where undertreatment hides. Fewer units cost less and wear off faster, which is fine if you were told that's what you were buying.</p><p>Some treatments are priced whole because the dose is predictable:</p><ul><li><p>Lip flip — ₦120,000</p></li><li><p>Brow lift — ₦45,000</p></li><li><p>Gummy smile correction — ₦200,000</p></li><li><p>Masseter slimming, for jaw width or teeth grinding — ₦300,000</p></li></ul><p>Masseter treatment is worth calling out because people come for the jawline and stay for the grinding. If you wake with jaw ache or your dentist has mentioned wear on your molars, relaxing that muscle treats the cause, and the narrower jaw is a side effect.</p><h2>What happens at the appointment</h2><p>A first appointment runs about thirty minutes, most of it talking. You'll be asked to frown, raise your brows, and smile so your injector can see which muscles are doing what, and how strong they are. Medical history matters here: pregnancy, breastfeeding, neuromuscular conditions, and some antibiotics all rule treatment out or change it.</p><p>The injections themselves take a few minutes. The needle is very fine. Most people rate it well below a blood test, and no numbing is needed for standard areas. You may see small raised bumps where the product went in; those settle within twenty minutes.</p><p>You can drive yourself home and go back to work. There's no sedation and nothing to recover from.</p><h2>The first two weeks</h2><p>Nothing happens on day one, and people who don't know this spend the evening convinced it failed. The effect starts appearing around day three, builds through the first week, and reaches full strength at day fourteen.</p><p>That two-week mark is when your result should be reviewed. Judging it earlier is how people end up overtreated: you decide at day five that it isn't working, ask for more, and then the original dose arrives on top of the extra one.</p><p>For the first four hours, stay upright and don't rub the treated area. Skip the gym, alcohol, and heat until the next day. These reduce the chance of the product spreading somewhere it wasn't meant to go.</p><h2>How long it lasts</h2><p>Three to four months is the usual range. Strong muscles need retreating sooner. So does a fast metabolism, and so does heavy training.</p><p>The return of movement is gradual. Rather than snapping back, the muscle recovers over a few weeks, which is why regular patients book their next appointment while they still have some effect left. Treating consistently over a couple of years often means needing fewer units, because a muscle that's been relaxed for long stretches gets weaker.</p><h2>Choosing an injector</h2><p>The Lagos market has a wide spread of skill, and price alone won't tell you where someone sits on it. Some questions worth asking:</p><ul><li><p>Who is injecting me, and what is their medical qualification?</p></li><li><p>How many units am I getting, and in which muscles?</p></li><li><p>What product are you using, and can I see the vial?</p></li><li><p>What happens if my brow drops or one side lifts more than the other?</p></li><li><p>Is the two-week review included?</p></li></ul><p>That last pair matters most. Complications from Botox are usually temporary, but they need someone who recognises them and knows what to do. A dropped eyelid can be treated with prescription drops. Asymmetry can often be balanced with a few extra units. Someone who can't answer what they'd do in those situations shouldn't be holding the needle.</p><p>Ask to see the vial. It costs nothing, and it's the simplest check against counterfeit product, which does circulate.</p><h2>When the answer is no</h2><p>A clinic that treats everyone who walks in isn't being generous. If your lines are static, if you've had a poor result elsewhere that hasn't worn off yet, or if what's bothering you is really volume or skin quality, Botox is the wrong tool and you'll have spent money to be disappointed.</p><p>Bring a photograph of what's bothering you if you can, and say what you want to look like rather than which treatment you want. Sometimes the answer is a smaller intervention than you expected. Sometimes it's nothing at all.</p>$body_html$,
